// @check
// @error parameter `a` default: expected string, got i32
// @error argument 1: expected string, got i32
// The default is checked against its own parameter's annotation at the
// DECLARATION, so a mismatched default is reported AT ITSELF and not only as
// somebody else's bad argument. It is then checked again as the argument it
// becomes — the second error — because the sugar is a substitution and the
// substituted program is what has to type-check.
function h(a: string = 5): string { a }
print(h())
